                    <h3 class="text-white font-black text-xl leading-tight m-0">
                      {index + 1}. {product.name}
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                  </div>
                  <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
                    {product.badge}
                  </span>
                </div>
                <div class="p-6">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4 flex-wrap">
                    <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Typical street price</span>
                    <span class="ml-auto font-black text-gray-900">{product.priceRange}</span>
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400 mb-4">{product.specs}</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6" set:html={product.description} />
                  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <h4 class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mt-0 mb-2">What We Like</h4>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.pros.map((pro) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{pro}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <h4 class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mt-0 mb-2">What We Don't Like</h4>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.cons.map((con) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{con}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="border-t border-gray-100 pt-4">
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold mb-4">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                      <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
                        {product.amazonLabel} &rarr;
                      </a>
                      {product.reviewUrl && (
                        <a href={product.reviewUrl} class="text-[#0F62FE] font-bold no-underline text-sm hover:underline">
                          Read our full {product.name} review &rarr;
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="who-makes-spirit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Makes Spirit Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            Spirit Fitness is owned by Dyaco International, a fitness equipment company based in
            Taipei. Dyaco's own corporate timeline records that it acquired Spirit Fitness, "a
            well-known fitness brand headquartered in Jonesboro, Arkansas", in 2008, and a trade
            announcement at the time described Spirit as a maker of home and light commercial
            fitness equipment since 1986. Spirit's own material elsewhere dates its first product to
            1983, so the founding year depends on whose account you read.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where they are made</h3>
          <p>
            The same 2008 announcement said Spirit would stop domestic manufacturing and keep its
            sales, marketing, warehousing, distribution and customer service in Jonesboro. Dyaco's
            timeline records a full logistics centre opening there in 2012. Independent review
            coverage describes current Spirit treadmills as made in Taiwan, where Dyaco is based.
            Spirit is therefore an American-run brand with Taiwanese manufacturing, which is the
            usual arrangement in this part of the market.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Spirit, Sole and XTERRA</h3>
          <p>
            Dyaco's timeline also records an international distribution agreement with Sole Fitness
            in 2010 and the acquisition of the XTERRA Fitness brand rights in 2013. In practice that
            means a Spirit and a Sole are cousins rather than strangers: they come from the same
            corporate family, are sold through overlapping dealer networks, and compete on similar
            terms. That is useful to know when a dealer recommends one over the other, and it is
            why the comparison later on this page is closer than it looks from the brand names. Our
            <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole roundup</a>
            covers the other side of the family.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not confuse Spirit Fitness with Sears' old Free Spirit label, which was a different
            line of treadmills sold under Sears' own branding. If you are trying to find parts for a
            Free Spirit, the model number on the frame label is what matters, not the word Spirit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Current Spirit Treadmill Range</h2>
<p>
            On 14 October 2026 Spirit's US site listed five residential treadmills in its XT range,
            two of them also offered as ENT versions with a touchscreen, plus commercial and
            rehabilitation treadmills sold to facilities. The table gives the residential range with
            the figures on which Spirit and at least one independent source agree.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Frame</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Deck</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Motor</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Max user</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Spirit price</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">XT185</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 55 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sources conflict</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">275 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,199.99</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">XT285</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.25 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sources conflict</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,599.00</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">XT385</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.5 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">375 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,699.99</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">XT485</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.0 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">425 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,949.99</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">XT685</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Non-folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.0 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">425 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,199.99</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two cells say "sources conflict", and they are worth explaining. For the XT185, Spirit
            lists a 3.0 HP motor, one review gives 2.75 CHP and another 2.5 HP, and one reviewer
            measured a shorter deck than the 20 by 55 inches Spirit and a retailer give — likely a
            mix of model years. For the XT285, Spirit lists 350 lb and a retailer comparison table
            325 lb. Retailer tables also give older, lower weight ratings for the XT485 and XT685
            than Spirit's current 425 lb, which is consistent with the ratings having risen between
            generations. The figure on the frame label of the unit you buy is the one that counts.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Speed and incline</h3>
          <p>
            Every model except the XT185 runs from 0.5 to 12 mph; the XT185 tops out at 10 mph. Spirit
            expresses incline in levels rather than percent — 10 on the XT185, 12 on the XT285 and 15
            on the XT385, XT485 and XT685, each in half-level steps. Independent coverage of the XT685
            describes its top setting as 15%, but because Spirit does not state a percentage on the
            listing, check the manual if an exact grade matters to your training plan.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">ENT, commercial and rehabilitation models</h3>
          <p>
            The XT485ENT and XT685ENT add a touchscreen; Spirit listed the XT685ENT, with a
            15.6-inch screen, at $3,899.99. Above the XT range, Spirit sells CT800, CT850, CT900 and
            CT1000ENT commercial treadmills and a rehabilitation line to facilities. The CT models
            are club machines with commercial warranties; if you are tempted to put one in a home,
            read our guide to
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym treadmills at home</a>
            first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="xt485-vs-xt685" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Spirit XT485 vs XT685: Fold or Fixed?</h2>
<p>
            Buy the XT685 if the treadmill will stay put; buy the XT485 if it has to fold. On the
            figures that decide most purchases they are identical: a 22 by 60-inch deck, a 4.0 HP
            motor, 0.5 to 12 mph, 15 incline levels and a 425 lb user rating. The price difference
            on Spirit's site was $250.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the extra $250 buys</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A fixed frame.</strong> A non-folding frame has no hinge or lift mechanism, which is the usual reason heavy users and daily runners prefer one.</li>
            <li><strong>Larger rollers.</strong> Spirit lists 3-inch front and rear rollers on the XT685 and 2.5-inch rollers on the XT485. Larger rollers turn more slowly for the same belt speed, which generally means less wear on the belt and bearings.</li>
            <li><strong>Light commercial cover.</strong> The XT685 is the only XT treadmill Spirit also warrants for non-dues facilities such as apartment-building gyms, on shorter terms: lifetime frame, five years on the motor, three on deck and parts, one on labour.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the XT485 keeps</h3>
          <p>
            The fold. Spirit lists the XT485 at 43 inches long folded, though it remains 71.7 inches
            tall and weighs about 289 lb, so folding saves floor space rather than making it
            portable. If you need the floor back after each session, that is decisive; if not, the
            XT685 is the better machine for the same running.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both are among the heavier-duty options at their price for users near the upper weight
            ratings. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills for heavier users</a>
            sets them against the alternatives, and our
            <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding treadmill picks</a>
            put the XT485 in context.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Spirit Warranty, Read Properly</h2>
<p>
            Every residential XT treadmill on Spirit's US site carries the same headline terms:
            lifetime on the frame, motor and deck, ten years on parts and one year of labour. That
            is long cover by the standards of the price band, and the ten years on parts in
            particular is longer than many rivals offer.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The short part</h3>
          <p>
            One year of labour is the term to plan around. From the second year, a covered part may
            arrive free while the visit to fit it does not. Independent coverage of the XT485 makes
            the same point. Many treadmill repairs are within reach of a careful owner with the
            manual, but some are not, so price a technician's call-out where you live before
            deciding it does not matter.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dealers and service</h3>
          <p>
            Spirit sells through a dealer network as well as its own online store, and it asks
            owners to register the product. When you buy, ask who carries out warranty service in
            your area and how a claim is handled — through the dealer, through Spirit's customer
            service in Jonesboro, or both. The dealer relationship is part of what you are paying
            for with a dealer-sold brand. Spirit's XT685 page also notes that prisons and
            correctional facilities are excluded from warranty cover, which is a reminder that the
            residential terms assume ordinary home use.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            covers what counts as a part versus a wear item, and why a lubrication log helps if you
            ever need to claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apps-and-zwift" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Does a Spirit Treadmill Need a Subscription?</h2>
<p>
            No. Every Spirit treadmill works on its own console, and the app side is optional.
            Spirit's own app, SPIRIT+, offers on-demand classes, and Spirit says a qualifying
            equipment purchase includes a free membership; without one it is a monthly
            subscription, and a serial number is needed to activate the free version. Check the
            current terms when you buy.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Zwift and Kinomap</h3>
          <p>
            The XT285 and above have built-in Bluetooth FTMS, the standard fitness-machine
            protocol, and Spirit's product pages name Zwift and Kinomap; Spirit's connectivity page
            includes a guide to pairing with Zwift. Zwift's own list of manufacturers with confirmed
            compatible models includes Spirit, and Zwift says compatibility can vary by model and
            production year. In practice the treadmill sends its speed to Zwift; you change the
            incline yourself. Our
            <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            covers set-up and pairing problems, and the XT185, which Spirit does not list with FTMS,
            would need a foot pod to run Zwift.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why this matters for value</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill that works fully without a membership costs the same in year five as in
            year one, and holds its value better second-hand, because the next owner does not
            inherit a platform decision. That is a real advantage over screen-led machines that do
            much less once the trial ends. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            sets out the arithmetic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spirit-vs-sole" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Spirit vs Sole: Which Should You Buy?</h2>
<p>
            Decide on the delivered price, the dealer and the console, because the two brands are
            close on paper and share a parent company. Sole's own site publishes head-to-head
            comparisons of the XT485 with its F80 and of the XT685 with its TT8, which tells you who
            each brand sees as its rival, and both brands lead with long frame and motor cover and
            consoles that need no membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            The differences are in the detail. Spirit's residential parts term is ten years across
            the XT range. Sole's terms vary by model and have changed between model years, and our
            pages on the Sole F80 and F85 are held back as drafts precisely because current Sole
            figures conflict across sources, so get the specific Sole model's terms in writing.
            Sole sells direct online and through large retailers such as Dick's Sporting Goods; Spirit leans on specialist dealers,
            which can mean better advice and local service but less aggressive discounting.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to decide</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Price on the day.</strong> Compare the delivered total, including assembly.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty line by line.</strong> Frame, motor, deck, parts and labour, for the exact model.</li>
            <li><strong>The console.</strong> Walk on both if you can and use the controls at your real speeds.</li>
            <li><strong>Service.</strong> Ask each seller who repairs it where you live.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/sole-vs-nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole vs NordicTrack comparison</a>
            and the <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole roundup</a>
            give the Sole side in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strengths-and-weaknesses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Spirit Does Well, and Less Well</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does well</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Long parts cover.</strong> Ten years on parts with lifetime frame, motor and deck is strong for the price band.</li>
            <li><strong>Full-size decks.</strong> Three of the five XT models have a 22 by 60-inch deck, the size tall runners and heavier users are usually pointed to.</li>
            <li><strong>No lock-in.</strong> Plain consoles, standard Bluetooth, and an optional app.</li>
            <li><strong>High weight ratings.</strong> 425 lb on the XT485 and XT685.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does less well</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Labour.</strong> One year across the range.</li>
            <li><strong>Noise.</strong> The most repeated complaint in owner and reviewer comments on the XT685, which is worth weighing if the treadmill is going above a bedroom or in a flat.</li>
            <li><strong>Motor labelling.</strong> Spirit lists horsepower without saying whether it is a continuous-duty figure, so the number cannot be compared directly with another brand's CHP.</li>
            <li><strong>Availability.</strong> Dealer-led distribution means fewer places to see one and less frequent deep discounts.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why the motor warranty term — lifetime, on Spirit's residential treadmills — is
            the more useful signal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Where to Buy a Spirit Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Spirit sells through its own US online store and through a network of specialist dealers
            found with its store locator. Larger independent retailers such as Abt also list Spirit
            treadmills. Spirit is less common on Amazon and in sporting-goods chains than the
            mass-market brands, so a search there may show limited stock or third-party sellers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Buying from a local dealer is worth considering with Spirit in particular. You can walk
            on the machine, the dealer can arrange delivery and assembly, and you have a named
            business to call when something needs a technician. Ask whether assembly is included,
            whether old equipment is taken away, and what the return terms are — dealer policies
            differ more than online ones.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Buying used</h3>
          <p>
            Older Spirit treadmills turn up second-hand, and the brand's reputation for durable
            frames makes them reasonable buys when priced to match. Model numbers have been reused
            across generations — the XT485 has been sold since at least 2014 — so the serial label
            matters more than the name in the listing. Assume no warranty transfers, test the
            incline through its full range and listen to the motor at your running speed. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            covers the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-spirit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Which Spirit Treadmill Should You Buy?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Most buyers: the XT485 or the XT685.</strong> They are the two models with the
            strongest figures and the most independent coverage. Choose the XT685 for a permanent
            space and heavy use, the XT485 if it must fold.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walkers on a tighter budget: the XT285 or XT385.</strong> The XT385 keeps the
            22-inch deck at a lower price and a 375 lb rating; the XT285 has a 20-inch deck. Both
            fold and have Bluetooth FTMS.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Short users and walkers only: the XT185, with care.</strong> Its 55-inch deck,
            10 mph top speed and lack of FTMS make it a walking machine, and its motor figures
            conflict between sources.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anyone who wants a screen: the ENT versions</strong>, or a machine built around
            a platform, priced over five years. For more choice at the top end, our
            <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">over-$2,500 roundup</a>
            and the <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Landice brand guide</a>
            cover heavier machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Spirit treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Spirit Fitness, based in Jonesboro, Arkansas. It has been owned since 2008 by Dyaco International, a Taiwanese fitness company that also distributes Sole and owns the XTERRA brand rights. Spirit stopped manufacturing in the US after the acquisition and kept sales, distribution and customer service in Jonesboro; independent coverage says the treadmills are made in Taiwan.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Spirit XT485 a good treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On its published figures, yes. Spirit and independent sources agree on a 22 by 60-inch deck, a 4.0 HP motor, 12 mph, 15 incline levels and a 425 lb rating, on a folding frame, with lifetime frame, motor and deck cover and ten years on parts. The caveats are one year of labour and a weight of about 289 lb. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Spirit XT485 and XT685?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The XT485 folds and the XT685 does not. Their deck, motor, speed, incline and weight rating figures match. The XT685 adds 3-inch rollers instead of 2.5-inch, a light commercial warranty option for non-dues facilities, and costs about $250 more on Spirit’s site. Choose the XT685 for a permanent space and the XT485 if floor space matters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Spirit treadmills work with Zwift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do. The XT285, XT385, XT485 and XT685 have built-in Bluetooth FTMS, and Spirit’s pages name Zwift and Kinomap. Zwift’s list of manufacturers with confirmed compatible models includes Spirit, though it says compatibility varies by model and year. The treadmill sends speed to Zwift; you set the incline yourself. The XT185 would need a foot pod.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need a subscription for a Spirit treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Every Spirit console works without one. Spirit’s SPIRIT+ app of on-demand classes is free with a qualifying equipment purchase, activated with the serial number, and otherwise a monthly subscription. Third-party apps such as Zwift and Kinomap charge separately. The treadmill’s speed, incline and programmes all work on their own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty do Spirit treadmills have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On Spirit’s US site every residential XT treadmill carried lifetime cover on the frame, motor and deck, ten years on parts and one year of labour on 14 October 2026. The XT685 also has light commercial terms for non-dues facilities. Register the product, keep proof of purchase, and ask the dealer who carries out service locally.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Spirit Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Spirit treadmills are well specified for the money. These are the errors that undo that.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting an old weight rating, or a new one, from a listing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Retailer tables give lower weight ratings for the XT485 and XT685 than Spirit’s current 425 lb, and one retailer FAQ gave 350 lb for the XT485. Ratings appear to have changed between generations. If you are near the limit, check the label on the actual unit or ask the dealer to confirm the model year before you buy.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the one-year labour term</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Lifetime frame, motor and deck cover and ten years on parts are strong, but labour is one year. From year two a covered part may still mean paying for the visit. Ask the dealer what a call-out costs locally and whether they carry out service themselves.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the XT485 when the XT685 would never be folded</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The two share their key figures and differ by about $250. If the treadmill will stay in one place, the fixed frame and larger rollers of the XT685 are the better use of that money. Fold only if you will actually fold it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on brand name between Spirit and Sole</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Spirit and Sole share a parent company and compete closely. Compare the delivered price, the full warranty line by line for the exact models, the console and the local service arrangements, not the logo. The better buy on the day is often simply the one with the better delivered price and nearer service.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

